course: chinese-street
lesson: 30
title: Street chatter: wow, really?, cool!
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most of what you hear from Ming and his friends isn't sentences at all — it's little noises and quick words: āiyā!, wa!, zhēn de ma?, kù! Learn these and you sound like you belong, even with ten words of Chinese.
explain: āiyā («IGH-yah») is the famous one. Ming's granny drops a bowl — āiyā! His mum sees the muddy floor — āiyā! His dad hears good news — āiyā! It can be cross, surprised or fond, so you watch the face, not the word.
explain: Your friend says he got a hundred marks. You can gasp «zhēn de ma?» (really?) or «bù huì ba!» (no way!), and when he shows you the test, «lìhai!» or «tài niú le!» — literally «too cow», meaning awesome.
explain: Careful with the sound-alikes: kàn (look) falls, while dipping kǎn means chop. And nèige («NAY-guh», um, that...) is just a filler while thinking; it may remind an English ear of a rude word, but in Chinese it is completely innocent, so never laugh at it.
fact: āiyā («IGH-yah») means oh no, oh dear or oh my; everyone says it, and it can be cross, surprised or fond, so you read the face.
fact: wa («wah!», high and long) means wow, said just like in English when something is amazing.
fact: zhēn de ma («JUN duh mah») means really? are you serious?; zhēn de without ma means really, honestly!, as in wǒ zhēn de méi zuòbì (I honestly didn't cheat).
fact: bù huì ba («BOO HWAY ba») means no way, you're kidding; it is disbelief, not a refusal, and the usual reply is zhēn de!
fact: lìhai («LEE-high») means amazing, so good, as in nǐ hǎo lìhai!; it also means fierce, so a lìhai teacher is a strict one.
fact: tài niú le («TIGH NYO luh», literally «too cow») is kids' and teenagers' slang for awesome; to grandparents it may sound odd or cheeky, and niú alone is cow.
fact: kù («koo!», falling) means cool and was borrowed from English; hǎo kù! means so cool.
fact: suàn le («SWAHN luh») means forget it, never mind, as in suàn le, bù wánr le; it can be calm or hurt, so watch the friend's face.
fact: kuài kàn («KWIGH KAHN») means look, quick!; the answer is nǎr? (where?), and kàn falls, while dipping kǎn means chop.
fact: zěnme le («DZUN-muh luh») means what's up, what happened?; asking it gently is the kind thing when a friend is quiet, and the answer may be méi shìr or the real story.
fact: nǐ gàn má ne («nee GAHN MAH nuh») means what are you doing?, between friends and siblings; said sharply it sounds like «what do you think you're doing?!», so to grown-ups ask nǐ zài zuò shénme? instead.
fact: nèige («NAY-guh») means um, er, that..., a filler everyone uses while searching for a word; it may sound like a rude English word to some ears, but it is completely innocent and never to be mocked.
exercise: Ming's granny drops a bowl and cries out. What does she most likely say? | āiyā! | kù! | tài niú le! | wa!
exercise: How do you tell whether an āiyā is cross, surprised or fond? | Watch the face and the voice | Count the syllables in it, slowly | It is always cross | It is always fond, like a hug
exercise: Ming does a perfect kick-flip. Which sound is just like English? | wa! | āiyā! | suàn le | nèige
exercise: Ming says he saw a fox in the playground. How do you ask «really?» | zhēn de ma? | zěnme le? | kuài kàn! | suàn le!
exercise: What does zhēn de without ma mean? | Really, honestly! | Really? | Never mind | What's up?
exercise: «bù huì ba!» What is it? | No way, you're kidding! | No, I won't do it, ever | I can't swim, not at all | Goodbye
exercise: Ming scores from the halfway line. How do you praise him? | nǐ hǎo lìhai! | nǐ gàn má ne! | suàn le! | zěnme le?
exercise: A lìhai teacher is | Strict and fierce | Amazing at football | Always really funny | Very lazy in class
exercise: What does tài niú le literally mean? | Too cow | Too cool | Too fast | Too hot to touch
exercise: Who is tài niú le best kept for? | Friends and other kids | Grandparents at a big dinner | Teachers in class, to be polite | The head teacher in assembly
exercise: Where does kù come from? | It was borrowed from English «cool» | It means cow, a strong animal | It is the sound of a fast car going past | It is a granny's word
exercise: The game keeps going wrong and Ming sighs «suàn le, bù wánr le». What does he mean? | Forget it, I'm not playing any more | Let's play again, from the very start | It's my turn | I won
exercise: Ming points at the sky: «kuài kàn!». What do you answer? | nǎr? where? | bù huì ba | suàn le | zhēn de
exercise: kàn (look) falls. What does dipping kǎn mean? | Chop | See | Cow | Cool
exercise: Ming is sitting quietly by himself. What's a kind thing to ask? | zěnme le? gently | nǐ gàn má ne?! sharply | tài niú le! | kuài kàn!
exercise: Your little sister is drawing on the wall. What do you ask her? | nǐ gàn má ne? | nín xiān chī? | zhēn guāi? | kù?
exercise: How do you ask Ming's dad politely what he is doing? | nǐ zài zuò shénme? | nǐ gàn má ne?! | zěnme le, lǎo de? | nèige?
exercise: Ming's mum stops mid-sentence: «nèige... nèige...». What is she doing? | Searching for a word, like «um» | Saying something rude under her breath | Calling the dog | Telling you off
test: «my mate says āiyā only means you're angry» Is he right? | No, it can be cross, surprised or fond | Yes, only angry, every single time | Yes, only for grandparents | No, it only means wow
test: «ming swore he didn't cheat, he said wǒ zhēn de méi zuòbì» What did he say? | I honestly didn't cheat | Did I cheat? Who says so? | I really did cheat, sorry | I'll cheat next time, maybe
test: You tell Ming you met a famous footballer and he says «bù huì ba!». Is he refusing something? | No, it's disbelief: no way! | Yes, he won't believe you ever | Yes, he won't play | No, it means he's bored
test: «yo granny, ur dumplings are tài niú le» How might Granny take it? | It may sound odd or cheeky to her | It's the most polite praise | She'll think you mean a cow is in them | She'll say zhēn guāi at once
test: «my new teacher is SO lìhai» Ming groans. What does he probably mean? | She's strict and fierce | She's amazing at maths | She's very kind | She's very small
test: What would happen if you shouted «kuài kǎn!» with a dipping tone? | It'd sound like «quick, chop!», not «look!» | Nothing, both mean look | It would mean cool | It would mean cow, so everyone would look for one
test: «heard ming's mum say that word lol, sounded rude, gonna copy her» What should you know? | nèige is an innocent «um»; never mock it | It is a swear word in Chinese, a very bad one | It means stupid | It is only for teenagers
test: «why u snapping nǐ gàn má ne at me, I'm just sitting here» What went wrong? | Said sharply it sounds like «what are you doing?!» | It is always a kind question, so nothing went wrong | It means «go away» | It means «you're late»
test: Ming loses a game and mutters «suàn le» with a hurt face. What's going on? | He's upset: «whatever, forget it» | He's happy | He wants to play again at once, straight away | He's saying thank you
test: In a cartoon a kid says «kù!» with a rising tone and nobody understands. How is kù said? | Falling, like «KOO!» | Rising, like a question | Dipping down and long | With no real tone at all
test: «zěnme le?» «méi shìr» — but your friend is crying. What's kind? | Stay with him and ask again gently | Believe him and run off to play | Say «bù huì ba!» | Laugh and say kù, to cheer him up a bit
exam: Identify the interjection most commonly employed to express dismay, surprise or affection, according to context. | āiyā | kù | suàn le | nèige
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the expression bù huì ba. | It conveys incredulity and does not constitute a refusal | It constitutes a formal refusal of an invitation to play | It is an expression of gratitude | It is a reproach directed at elders
exam: Ascertain the dual signification of the term lìhai. | Admirable or impressive, and also fierce or strict | Cold and also hot, according to the season | Fast and also slow | Young and also old
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression nèige? | It is an innocent filler employed while seeking a word | It is an offensive term to be avoided in all company | It is a formal greeting to elders | It is a command to look
